The Hindu,South Asia,en,2 firefighting helicopters collide midair near Athens as Europe's wildfire emergency rages,2 firefighting helicopters collide midair near Athens as Europe's wildfire emergency rages,"2 firefighting helicopters collide midair near Athens as Europe's wildfire emergency rages The collision underscored the continuing danger at the most volatile front of Europe's wildfire emergency. You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Published- August 02, 2026 10:42 pm IST - Megara (Greece) Firefighters operate during a wildfire near Porto Germeno, northwest of Athens, Greece, Sunday, Aug. 2, 2026. | Photo Credit: AP Two firefighting helicopters collided on Sunday (August 2, 2026) while battling a wind-driven wildfire in the Porto Germeno area, west of Athens. Footage showed one of the aircraft crashing to the ground. Each helicopter was carrying a two-person crew, the Greek fire department said. A search-and-rescue operation was underway. Authorities did not immediately provide information on the crews' condition. The collision underscored the continuing danger at the most volatile front of Europe's wildfire emergency. The continent's crisis split into three stark fronts: strong winds drove flames deeper into the Athens region and forced fresh evacuations, France guarded a still-burning scar four times the size of Paris, and Spain's major fires stopped advancing. The most immediate danger was in Greece. Flames spread through western Attica, the region around Athens, threatening communities and an industrial district outsid","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 22:42:02 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/2-firefighting-helicopters-collide-midair-near-athens-as-europes-wildfire-emergency-rages/article71298781.ece,2026-08-03 01:30:52
The Hindu,South Asia,en,"Sheikh Hasina to hold presser on August 5, a month before Tarique Rahman’s expected visit","Sheikh Hasina to hold presser on August 5, a month before Tarique Rahman’s expected visit","Sheikh Hasina to hold presser on August 5, a month before Tarique Rahman’s expected visit Bangladesh’s deposed leader will address the media virtually on the second anniversary of her arrival in India post-2024 uprising You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- August 02, 2026 10:36 pm IST - New Delhi Bangladesh’s deposed Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina will address the international media virtually to announce her homecoming and outline her vision for Bangladesh. File. | Photo Credit: AFP A month before theexpected visit of Bangladesh Prime Minister Tarique Rahmanfor the India-chaired BRICS summit, the country’s deposed Prime MinisterSheikh Hasinawill address a press conference at the Foreign Correspondents Club in New Delhi on Wednesday (August 5, 2026), the second anniversary of her arrival in India following themonsoon uprising of 2024 in Dhaka. The media interaction will be addressed by Ms. Hasina’s son Sajeeb Wazed Joy and the former Education Minister of Bangladesh, Mohibul Hasan Chowdhury Nowfel. “In this exclusive engagement, Sheikh Hasina will address the international media virtually to announce her homecoming and outline her vision for Bangladesh. The session will also feature senior leaders of the Bangladesh Awami League and former Ministers, participating both in person and virtually, followed by an interactive Q&A,” said Waiel Aww","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 22:36:39 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/inter